From: keks2293 <rusik2293@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] regmap: debugfs: enable the 'registers' write path

The 'registers' debugfs file of a regmap is read-only by default:
both the write handler (regmap_map_write_file) and the 0600 file mode
are gated by REGMAP_ALLOW_WRITE_DEBUGFS, which the source
intentionally leaves undefined. Upstream documents the feature as
dangerous for regmaps of PMIC clients and deliberately provides no
compile-time option: "people who want to use this will need to modify
the source code directly".

On the RP6 (QCS8550) the eUSB2 repeater tuning registers of the
pm8550b (0xfd00-0xfd5b) must be writable at runtime from the
initramfs: in 'linux usb' boot mode the bootloader leaves its USB
host session residue in the tuning registers that the mainline
kernel no longer re-initializes (the zero-out was removed upstream in
commit 31bc94de), and testing candidate fix values (the pre-31bc94de
zero-out, or the hardware reset values captured on a working boot)
requires live regmap writes.

Define the guard at the exact line the source expects the
modification (the #undef that precedes both the write handler and
the file-mode selection), so the write handler is compiled in and
the 'registers' file is created 0600. Every write taints the kernel
(TAINT_USER) -- acceptable on this debug branch.
---
--- a/drivers/base/regmap/regmap-debugfs.c
+++ b/drivers/base/regmap/regmap-debugfs.c
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@
 				   count, ppos);
 }
 
-#undef REGMAP_ALLOW_WRITE_DEBUGFS
+#define REGMAP_ALLOW_WRITE_DEBUGFS
 #ifdef REGMAP_ALLOW_WRITE_DEBUGFS
 /*
  * This can be dangerous especially when we have clients such as
